# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: vl-exit-with-error-leaks-host-condition -- vl-exit-with-error outside a VLX crashes with an SBCL backtrace
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-10-21

* Symptom

Found while writing the functional-abstraction section of
=clautolisp/documentation/tutorial.org=, on clautolisp 2.3.24:

#+begin_example
$ clautolisp --no-init -x '(vl-exit-with-error "boom")'
Unhandled CLAUTOLISP.AUTOLISP-RUNTIME:AUTOLISP-NAMESPACE-EXIT in thread #<SB-THREAD:THREAD tid=259 "main thread" RUNNING ...>:
  AutoLISP namespace exit ERROR: "boom"
Backtrace for: #<SB-THREAD:THREAD ...>
0: (SB-DEBUG::DEBUGGER-DISABLED-HOOK ...)
...
unhandled condition in --disable-debugger mode, quitting
$ echo $?
1
#+end_example

Same with a global =*error*= defined: it is not called. Inside
=vl-catch-all-apply= it is caught correctly (=#<catch-all-error "boom">=).

* Expected

AGENTS.md: "Common Lisp conditions and errors must not leak directly into the
AutoLISP environment." =AUTOLISP-NAMESPACE-EXIT= signalled from the document
namespace (no separate VLX namespace to exit) escapes every handler of the
toplevel driver and reaches SBCL's debugger-disabled hook. It must instead be
handled like an AutoLISP error at the toplevel (message "boom", =*error*= /
=--on-error= policy applied), or like whatever the vendors do when
=vl-exit-with-error= is called outside a separate-namespace VLX -- to be
probed (AutoCAD documents it only for separate-namespace VLX applications).

* To do

1. Probe AutoCAD / BricsCAD: =(vl-exit-with-error "boom")= at the command line
   and from a plain =.lsp=; record the message and whether =*error*= runs.
2. Failing test, then handle =AUTOLISP-NAMESPACE-EXIT= at the toplevel (REPL,
   =-x=, =-l=) and in the debugger session.
3. Same check for =vl-exit-with-value=.

* Fix (2026-10-22, clautolisp 2.3.28 / alfe 2.3.28) -- DONE for the leak

- VL-EXIT-WITH-ERROR first SIGNALs AUTOLISP-NAMESPACE-EXIT, so a
  separate-namespace boundary (vl-catch-all-apply; clautolisp loads no VLX)
  still takes it; when none does -- the document namespace -- it raises
  the AutoLISP error :USER-ERROR with the message, at the call site, so
  *error* (a local one too), --on-error and the debugger see an ordinary
  error. -x: "clautolisp: runtime error: USER-ERROR: boom", status 1, no
  host backtrace; REPL: the debugger, as for any error; -l: the same.
- VL-EXIT-WITH-VALUE: CALL-WITH-AUTOLISP-ERROR-HANDLER (the toplevel of
  -x, -l forms and the REPL) handles the :VALUE exit -- the toplevel form
  is abandoned with that value.
- Tests (builtin-tests.lisp): VL-EXIT-WITH-ERROR-AT-TOPLEVEL-CALLS-ERROR-
  HANDLER (global and local *error*), VL-EXIT-WITH-ERROR-WITHOUT-HANDLER-IS-
  AN-AUTOLISP-ERROR, VL-EXIT-WITH-VALUE-AT-TOPLEVEL-IS-THE-FORMS-VALUE (and
  both unchanged inside vl-catch-all-apply). Against 2.3.27 the first one
  killed the test process with this ticket's very symptom (Unhandled
  AUTOLISP-NAMESPACE-EXIT).
- autolisp-spec: *** clautolisp sections for VL-EXIT-WITH-ERROR and
  VL-EXIT-WITH-VALUE.

Measured (control-flow probe, MR !471, job 17396028047, BricsCAD V26
macOS), inside vl-catch-all-apply: BricsCAD gives ERROR "" -- an empty
message -- for vl-exit-with-error AND vl-exit-with-value outside a VLX.
That vendor-semantics question is NOT this ticket (no vendor leaks a host
condition): it moves to vl-exit-outside-vlx-caught-diverges, pending the
AutoCAD measurement.
